/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A level surface is everywhere at right angles to gravity, like the surface of still water; because the earth is round, it is curved.
   - A horizontal plane at a point is the flat plane that touches the level surface at that point.
   - The datum is the level surface from which heights are measured.
   - The reduced level (RL) of a point is its height above the datum, in metres, usually written to three decimals (millimetres).
   - A benchmark (BM) is a fixed, well-marked point whose RL is known.
   - A station is the point where the staff is held and a reading is taken.
   - Each set-up of the level has one backsight (BS): the first reading, taken on a point of known RL.
   - The height of instrument (HI), also called the height of collimation, is the RL of the line of collimation for one set-up.
   - All values are in metres.
   - Combining the two gives RL(new) = RL(known) + BS − FS.
*/
