/* book exam traps: Taking the difference from the second set-up as the true one. Only the midpoint readings give the true difference, because equal sights cancel the collimation error. | Correcting the whole bubble error with the capstan screws. In the bubble tube adjustment, half the error is the instrument's (capstan screws) and half is the levelling (foot screws). | Doing the two-peg test with parallax or a moving bubble. Temporary adjustments come first, or a correct instrument gets “adjusted” wrongly. | Naming the wrong order. Permanent adjustments of a dumpy level: bubble tube first, then the cross-hair ring, then the line of collimation. */
